T1  - Association between iron metabolism biomarkers and all-cause mortality in critically ill patients with sepsis: a retrospective cohort study.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.cca.2026.122897
A1  - Gao, Liang
A1  - Liu, Junjin
A1  - Hu, Yuekai
A1  - Fan, Qichao
A1  - Cao, Jin
A1  - Huang, Jiaofeng
A1  - Ding, Haibo
Y1  - 2026//
Y2  - 20261013//
N2  - BACKGROUND: Iron metabolism disorders were frequently observed in sepsis, yet their association with outcomes remained incompletely understood. Therefore, this study aimed to evaluate the prognostic value of iron-related biomarkers and to develop models for predicting 30-day mortality in septic patients., METHODS: This retrospective cohort study analyzed 2729 sepsis patients from the Medical Information Mart for Intensive Care IV database to evaluate the prognostic value of iron metabolism biomarkers and to develop machine learning-based prediction models for 30-day mortality. We assessed associations using Kaplan-Meier analysis, multivariable Cox regression, and restricted cubic spline regression. Six machine learning models were developed and internally validated using 100 bootstrap resamples., RESULTS: The 30-day mortality rate was 19.57%. Ferritin levels above 680ng/mL were associated with the strongest mortality risk increase, while transferrin above 160mg/dL demonstrated a protective effect. Restricted cubic spline analysis revealed nonlinear relationships, with serum iron exhibiting an inverted U-shaped association between 35 and 328mcg/dL. The Light Gradient Boosting Machine model incorporating iron biomarkers achieved a validation area under the receiver operating characteristic curve of 0.782 (0.743-0.819), with significant reclassification improvement (Net Reclassification Improvement: 0.076, p=0.006)., CONCLUSION: Iron metabolism biomarkers provide significant incremental prognostic value for 30-day mortality in sepsis patients. Integrating them into machine learning models enhances risk stratification beyond traditional clinical scores, offering a clinically actionable tool for early identification of high-risk patients. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier B.V.
