T1  - Predicting disease risks by matching quantiles estimation for censored data.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.3934/mbe.2020251
A1  - Wu, Peng
A1  - Liang, Bao Sheng
A1  - Xia, Yi Fan
A1  - Tong, Xing Wei
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - In time to event data analysis, it is often of interest to predict quantities such as t-year survival rate or the survival function over a continuum of time. A commonly used approach is to relate the survival time to the covariates by a semiparametric regression model and then use the fitted model for prediction, which usually results in direct estimation of the conditional hazard function or the conditional estimating equation. Its prediction accuracy, however, relies on the correct specification of the covariate-survival association which is often difficult in practice, especially when patient populations are heterogeneous or the underlying model is complex. In this paper, from a prediction perspective, we propose a disease-risk prediction approach by matching an optimal combination of covariates with the survival time in terms of distribution quantiles. The proposed method is easy to implement and works flexibly without assuming a priori model. The redistribution-of-mass technique is adopted to accommodate censoring. We establish theoretical properties of the proposed method. Simulation studies and a real data example are also provided to further illustrate its practical utilities.
