T1  - Diagnostic potential of stored dried blood spots for inborn errors of metabolism: a metabolic autopsy of medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1136/jclinpath-2017-204962
A1  - Kaku, Noriyuki
A1  - Ihara, Kenji
A1  - Hirata, Yuichiro
A1  - Yamada, Kenji
A1  - Lee, Sooyoung
A1  - Kanemasa, Hikaru
A1  - Motomura, Yoshitomo
A1  - Baba, Haruhisa
A1  - Tanaka, Tamami
A1  - Sakai, Yasunari
A1  - Maehara, Yoshihiko
A1  - Ohga, Shouichi
Y1  - 2018//
Y2  - 20180502//
N2  - AIM: It is estimated that 1-5% of sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS) cases might be caused by undiagnosed inborn errors of metabolism (IEMs); however, the postmortem identification of IEMs remains difficult. This study aimed to evaluate the usefulness of dried blood spots (DBSs) stored after newborn screening tests as a metabolic autopsy to determine the causes of death in infants and children who died suddenly and unexpectedly., METHODS: Infants or toddlers who had suddenly died without a definite diagnosis between July 2008 and December 2012 at Kyushu University Hospital in Japan were enrolled in this study. Their Guthrie cards, which had been stored for several years at 4-8degreeC, were used for an acylcarnitine analysis by tandem mass spectrometry to identify inborn errors of metabolism., RESULTS: Fifteen infants and children who died at less than 2 years of age and for whom the cause of death was unknown were enrolled for the study. After correcting the C0 and C8 values assuming the hydrolysation of acylcarnitine in the stored DBSs, the corrected C8 value of one case just exceeded the cut-off level for medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (MCAD) deficiency screening. Genetic and biochemical analyses confirmed this patient to have MCAD deficiency., CONCLUSION: DBSs stored after newborn screening tests are a promising tool for metabolic autopsy. The appropriate compensation of acylcarnitine data and subsequent genetic and biochemical analyses are essential for the postmortem diagnosis of inborn errors of metabolism. Copyright © Article author(s) (or their employer(s) unless otherwise stated in the text of the article) 2018. All rights reserved. No commercial use is permitted unless otherwise expressly granted.
