T1  - Fungal endocarditis: an autopsy study.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1177/021849230401200202
A1  - Challa, Sundaram
A1  - Prayaga, Aruna Kumari
A1  - Vemu, Laxmi
A1  - Sadasivan, Jaishankar
A1  - Jagarlapudi, Murali Krishna Murthy
A1  - Digumarti, Raghunadharao
A1  - Prabhala, Rajagopal
Y1  - 2004//
N2  - Between 1990 and 2002, 237 hearts were examined at autopsy, including 16 with infective endocarditis; 6 showed fungal endocarditis. The preceding pathology was chronic rheumatic heart disease in 2 patients, one of whom had undergone double valve replacement, 2 patients had been treated for acute lymphoblastic leukemia, and one had protein-energy malnutrition. The underlying cause was unknown in one case. The organisms isolated were Aspergillus in 3 patients, Zygomycota in 1, Candida in 1, and both Candida tropicalis and Aspergillus in 1 patient. Immunosuppressed states are a cause of fungal endocarditis in India, although chronic rheumatic heart disease is the preceding pathology in the majority of patients.
